okay let's now dig further into the idea  of what government should and shouldn't  be involved in which is a really major  part of the conversation about police  departments we've already talked about  calls to abolish or defund the police in  light of the protests and the killing of  George Floyd there's a bigger question  implicit in that discussion which is  what should governments be involved in  and what shouldn't governments be  involved in and this is a question that  really tracks political views because on  one side you have extreme small  government conservatives and  libertarians they will sometimes go as  far as to say roads should be privatized  you can pay the owner to use whichever  road you want or look for a different  Road another example would be the  privatization of law enforcement where  if you want to be able to call the  police you pay some kind of a membership  fee to the law enforcement company that  you prefer Google cops or Amazon police  or whatever these are extreme examples  but there are some small government  libertarians that believe this stuff on  the extreme other hand you have truly  communist governments where the  government would potentially own all of  the industry and be the sole employer  they manufacture the vehicles the  government employs the doctors owns the  hospitals owns every restaurant decides  what food rations people get and what  farmers need to do with the crops that  they grow these are two extremes we have  no examples of success in the modern  world for either of the two extremes  because like with most things the devil  is in the details this is gray area so  let's dig into it my starting point as a  Social Democrat is that there are many  things government should be doing  instead of private industry but we  should be able to articulate a  compelling reason for why the government  should be involved instead of private  industry so in other words government  should not be the only provider of a  good or service unless there is a  specific case about why they should be  one of the most important things that  governments should be doing in  industries where they are not  participating is setting the ground  rules which I call regulation  I don't need government providing for  example car insurance but I do want  government saying to private businesses  if you want to be in the market of  offering car insurance here are the  rules you have to abide by similarly I  don't want the government running  Airlines and making the airplanes but I  do want government heavily involved in  saying if you're going to build  airplanes here are the ground rules if  you are going to sell tickets to fly  people around here are the ground rules  you need to adhere to now most examples  are pretty easy to get agreement on for  example very few people believe the  government should be the sole  manufacturer of mobile phones and we've  got plenty of companies Samsung Apple  and others that are innovating in that  space and making mobile phones and  that's fine but we'll get back to mobile  phones because there are complexities  that come in when you introduce  externalities which justify the role of  government now on the other hand most  people do believe that government should  be responsible for police departments  and law enforcement missile defense  defending the country from a foreign  attackers stopping terrorism etc and as  I mentioned in the other segment you  might think that it's a good idea to  leave something like human trafficking  to nonprofits and community groups but  this is stuff with such a massive legal  infrastructure and it's so big and  wide-ranging  I do want a properly functioning  government law enforcement apparatus to  be dealing with something like human  trafficking correctly so the small  government versus big government ideas  are very often theoretical they're about  principles but you've got to do way more  work to really figure out how our  economy should be organized health care  is something I want government more  involved in health care there are  examples from all over the world of  government being involved in health care  and a health care being better health  care is an exception as I mentioned we  need a compelling reason to say this  should be pulled out of for-profit  markets when we're talking about  people's health I don't believe at a  moral level that that should be  something subject to the whims a  companies looking to make profits so as  a social democrat i would say healthcare  meets the requirement to be pulled out  of for-profit market-based and then  you've got gray area so like i don't  need the government running all the  hospitals or employing all the doctors  necessarily but maybe I just want them  being the insurance company negotiating  prices establishing ground rules okay  that that's something that that I would  want let me give you another gray area I  want the government doing lots of  infrastructure spending I want them  improving our airports and our roads and  our bridges I don't need the government  running the construction companies or  being the construction company and  buying the bulldozers and the skid  steers and doing it all themselves I'm  fine with government hiring private  industry to do the actual infrastructure  work the problem is when the process of  hiring private industry becomes a crony  capitalist snow job where the jobs are  given to the president's friends so I'm  a social democrat I do think that  markets when they actually are well  regulated and function fairly direct  resources where they are most useful  from a utility point of view and  sometimes we have to say this area of  society should not be subjected to the  market calculation of where the most  utility is derived health being one of  those examples I don't believe for a  second this BS that we hear about  industries have incentives to regulate  themselves government doesn't need to  regulate because if the business does  something bad the PR will be terrible  news reports about them will make them  lose money just doesn't work doesn't  work consider a company that makes cars  and they pollute and harm some town  that's downwind and people in that town  get way more lung cancer over the twenty  years after the cars have been  manufactured that's an externality now  you could say well the bad PR of people  hearing about that is going to make  fewer people buy that company's cars and  so the company has an incentive not to  pollute in order to keep selling more  cars and avoid the bad PR just doesn't  happen these externalities are only  often loosely assignable to  specific action by a company often times  even if the company gets sued it is  cheaper to pollute and kill a bunch of  people and every once in a while you  just pay some civil lawsuit so the point  here is the externalities are not  properly dealt with by markets and that  is the role of government regulation so  the takeaway is I want most areas of  life operating in well regulated markets  with the profits properly taxed to  ensure that everyone has a decent  standard of living  I want industry to operate in a market  with enough safeguards and  infrastructure and it is not to their  advantage to hurt people in order to  earn the profits that they are earning  that's why the regulation has to be  strict enough and in some cases I  actually want the government  participating in the market rather than  just regulating it health care being an  exception an example of that so this  stuff is complex the principles people  who talk about liberty and  non-aggression and they just want to  talk abstractly they often don't want to  do or aren't able to do the difficult  work of really digging into the  specifics and you have to do it in order  to really get anywhere on this stuff  we're gonna continue talking about this  on the david pakman show instagram page  at david pakman show make sure you're  following us there
 

